From: SamuriHL <samurihl@msn.com>
Subject: drm: only force the DV tunnel wire format when the output is DV

meson_hdmitx_decide_color_attr forces the Dolby Vision tunnelling wire
format (RGB/444 8-bit for STD, YUV422 12-bit for LL) whenever the amdv
core is enabled. That predicate is too broad: the DV core can be enabled
while the OUTPUT on the wire is not Dolby Vision. Two such cases:

 - VS10 forces an HDR10/SDR output mode (FEL expand, DM tone-map) while
   dolby_vision_mode is HDR10/SDR10/SDR8; and
 - the disc-session native release (aml_dv_release_disc_engage) leaves the
   core enabled under FOLLOW_SOURCE and lets a selected non-DV title
   signal its own native format - e.g. picking the HDR10 demo from the
   Spears & Munsil UHD Benchmark's format-selector on an otherwise-DV disc.

Sequence on a VS10 DV->HDR10 playback: amdv engages HDR10 and hdmitx
accepts the DRM packet on the existing 10/12-bit wire (the HDR10 toast
the user briefly sees). ~1s later Kodi's refresh-rate switch triggers a
DRM modeset; decide_color_attr sees is_amdv_enable() and forces the wire
to YUV444 8-bit. The modeset teardown drops the DRM packet ("Dolby VSIF,
switching signal to SDR") and the new 8-bit wire makes
hdmitx_set_drm_pkt refuse every subsequent HDR10 packet (8-bit +
!config_csc_en -> forced BT709 SDR), so the signal latches on 8-bit SDR
for the rest of playback. Same mechanism for DV FEL and profile 8.1 on
every SoC family (hdmitx20 and hdmitx21).

The disc-session release hits the identical wire trap by a different door:
release_disc_engage sets dolby_vision_policy=FOLLOW_SOURCE and dv_mode=0
(target mode BYPASS) but does NOT clear dolby_vision_enable (the latch
keeps the core up across the session). So is_amdv_enable() stays true and
the DV wire is forced onto the native HDR10 title. On a lenient sink (LG)
the HDR10 packet survives; on a strict sink (Sony) the HDR10 signal on an
8-bit/444 DV wire renders as a magenta/pink cast. Switching back to a DV
title puts a real DV signal on the DV wire, so it looks correct again -
the reported DV->HDR10 pink / HDR10->DV recovery asymmetry.

Gate the two format-force sites on the amdv output being genuinely DV:
the core enabled, the policy actually FORCING an output (FOLLOW_SOURCE is
a native release, never a DV wire), and the target mode not one of the
non-DV VS10 outputs (HDR10/SDR10/SDR8). IPT/IPT_TUNNEL - and the transient
BYPASS while a FORCE engage is still resolving - keep the tunnel force
exactly as before; only the FOLLOW_SOURCE native release now yields the
native attr/EDID selection (e.g. 422,12bit) that carries HDR10 cleanly.

--- a/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/drm/meson_hdmi.c
@@ -286,6 +286,30 @@
 		return true;
 }
 
+/* The Dolby Vision tunnelling wire-format constraints (RGB/444 8-bit for
+ * STD, YUV422 12-bit for LL) only apply when the signal on the wire is
+ * actually Dolby Vision. The DV core can be enabled while the OUTPUT is
+ * not DV:
+ *  - VS10 forcing an HDR10/SDR output mode (FEL expand, DM tone-map), and
+ *  - the disc-session native release (aml_dv_release_disc_engage): the
+ *    core stays enabled under FOLLOW_SOURCE while a selected title signals
+ *    its own native HDR10 (S&M UHD Benchmark HDR10 demo off a DV disc).
+ * Both must keep the native format selection: an 8-bit/444 wire makes
+ * hdmitx refuse the HDR10 DRM packet, dropping it to SDR on lenient sinks
+ * or a magenta cast on strict sinks (Sony). FOLLOW_SOURCE never forces a
+ * DV wire; only FORCE_OUTPUT_MODE with a DV target does.
+ */
+static bool is_amdv_output_dv(void)
+{
+	int mode = get_amdv_target_mode();
+
+	return is_amdv_enable() &&
+	       get_amdv_policy() == AMDV_FORCE_OUTPUT_MODE &&
+	       mode != AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_HDR10 &&
+	       mode != AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_SDR10 &&
+	       mode != AMDV_OUTPUT_MODE_SDR8;
+}
+
 static int meson_hdmitx_decide_color_attr
 	(struct hdmitx_common *common, struct am_meson_crtc_state *crtc_state,
 	struct hdmitx_color_attr *attr, u64 sequence_id)
@@ -319,7 +336,7 @@
 	// try autoselect
 	// check if any colour subsampling is set
 	// force colour subsampling when DV mode
-	if (is_amdv_enable()) {
+	if (is_amdv_output_dv()) {
 		int cs = attr->colorformat;
 		if (get_amdv_ll_policy() == 0 /* DOLBY_VISION_LL_DISABLE */) {
 			attr->colorformat = HDMI_COLORSPACE_YUV444;
@@ -362,7 +379,7 @@
 
 	// check for bit colourdepth limit
 	// force bit colourdepth when DV mode
-	if (is_amdv_enable()) {
+	if (is_amdv_output_dv()) {
 		int cd = bitdepth_to_colordepth(attr->bitdepth);
 		if (get_amdv_ll_policy() == 0 /* DOLBY_VISION_LL_DISABLE */) {
 			attr->bitdepth = colordepth_to_bitdepth(COLORDEPTH_24B);
